a fake world with the real inputs. before the host will have you, write
the world it would hand you — the calls, the rows, the clock — and build
against that.

the test: name what the fake gets wrong. that list is the integration
risk and it is short, or you are not simulating the host, you are
imagining it.

the inputs are the deliverable, not the fake. capture them from the real
thing once — a recorded response, a dumped row, an actual file — then
freeze. invented inputs test the invention.

`debugging/reproduce:/on demand/` turned around: there you own the
trigger for a bug that exists, here the world for code that does not.
one property either way — it runs when you say so.

keep it running after integration or delete it. a simulator that rots is
a second truth and the wrong one; it lives beside the code, started by
the same command. `debugging/regression` is the case that earned a place
in it.

what it cannot fake is the integration — so integrate early and small,
and let the first real call correct the list.
